**user**:
> I have a dataframe in R, and I want to repeat the rows of it exactly, but with an extra column that iterates up from mc=1 to mc=1000 (so the result should also be 1000x bigger). Can I do that quickly with dplyr?
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can achieve this using `dplyr` in R. You can repeat the dataframe rows and add an additional column using `tidyr::crossing()` or `dplyr` in combination with `tidyverse` functions. Here's how you can do it:
> 
> ```r
> library(dplyr)
> library(tidyr)
> 
> # Assume df is your original dataframe
> df <- data.frame(x = 1:3, y = 4:6)  # Example dataframe
> 
> # Create a new dataframe with repeated rows and an additional column
> expanded_df <- df %>%
>   slice(rep(1:n(), each = 1000)) %>%
>   mutate(mc = rep(1:1000, times = n()))
> 
> # Alternatively, using tidyr::crossing
> expanded_df <- crossing(df, mc = 1:1000)
> 
> print(expanded_df)
> ```
> 
> Both methods will produce a dataframe where each original row is repeated 1000 times, and there's an additional column `mc` indicating the repetition number from 1 to 1000. The `crossing` function is particularly elegant as it directly creates all combinations.
